lesson.example:
**Fictional target:** https://target.example.com
**Vulnerable code (pseudo‑JavaScript):**
```js
app.post('/auth/v1/signup', (req, res) => {
  // No check that the request comes from an internal service
  const {email, password} = req.body;
  createUser(email, password).then(user => {
    res.json({role:'authenticated', email:user.email});
  });
});
```
**Attack request:**
```bash
curl -X POST "https://target.example.com/auth/v1/signup" \
  -H "apikey: sb_public_testkey" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email":"attacker@evil.com","password":"evilPass123"}'
```
**Why it works:** The server trusts any request that presents a valid Supabase API key and does not verify the caller’s identity, so the attacker can create a new account with the same privileges as a legitimate user. Once the account is created, the attacker can log in and access protected resources.

**Fictional target:** static.target.example
**Vulnerable snippet (client‑side JavaScript):**
```html
<script>
function check() {
  var pwd = document.getElementById('pwd').value;
  if (pwd == "Secret123") { // hard‑coded password
    window.location = '/secret.html';
  } else {
    alert('Wrong password');
  }
}
</script>
```
**Attack steps:**
1. Open the page in a browser and view the source (Ctrl+U). The script reveals the string "Secret123".
2. Enter "Secret123" into the password field and click submit.
3. The client‑side `if` statement evaluates to true, causing the browser to navigate to `/secret.html` and display the hidden content.
**Why it works:** The password check happens only in the browser; no server verification occurs, so anyone can read the script, learn the secret, and bypass authentication.

lesson.fix:
Move the password verification to the server side so the client never sees the secret. Store the secret in a secure location (environment variable, vault) and compare it on the backend, returning a session token on success. Remove any plain‑text passwords from JavaScript files. Example fix (Node/Express):
```js
app.post('/login', (req, res) => {
  const pwd = req.body.password;
  if (pwd === process.env.SECRET_PASSWORD) {
    // create authenticated session
    req.session.user = 'admin';
    res.send('OK');
  } else {
    res.status(401).send('Unauthorized');
  }
});
```
The server now performs the check, never exposes the secret to the browser, and enforces proper authentication flow.

